   103	\section{The new class}\label{sec1}
   104	In this section we will present a generalized version of the protocol presented in \cite{NSK}. 
   105	
   106	Let $S$ be a monoid and $\sim$ a finite index congruence on $S$. We will denote the class of an element $s\in S$ with respect to $\sim$ as $[s]$.
   107	
   108	\begin{definition}
   109	A morphism $\psi$ will be said to be $\sim$proper, if 
   110	\begin{itemize}
   111	\item $\psi\colon S\longrightarrow S$ is injective
   112	\item $\psi$ is compatible with $\sim$  (i.e. $\psi(x)\sim\psi(y)$ iff $x\sim y$)
   113	\item the induced application $\widetilde{\psi}\colon {S}/{\sim}\longrightarrow {S}/{\sim}$ is invertible.
   114	\end{itemize}
   115	\end{definition}
   116	
   117	\begin{definition}\label{defcryptable}
   118	Given $L\in\mathbb{N}$ we will say that $S$ is $L$-\emph{cryptable} under $\sim$ if there exists a $\sim$proper morphism $\psi$ and elements $s_1,\dots,s_L\in S$ such that
   119	\[\alpha_\sim^\psi: \vZ_2^L \longrightarrow S/\sim\]
   120	\[m=(m_1,\dots,m_L)\mapsto \left[\prod^L_{i=1}\psi(s_i)^{m_i}\right]\]
   121	is an injective application.
   122	\end{definition}
   123	
   124	
   125	
   126	The following proposition will be useful later on
   127	\begin{prop}\label{injalpha} Given a monoid $S$ that is $L$-cryptable under $\sim$, the following maps are also injective:
   128	\[\alpha^\psi: \vZ_2^L \longrightarrow S\]
   129	\[(m_1,\dots,m_L)\mapsto \prod^L_{i=1}\psi(s_i)^{m_i}\]
   130	\[\alpha_\sim: \vZ_2^L \longrightarrow S/\sim\]
   131	\[(m_1,\dots,m_L)\mapsto \left[\prod^L_{i=1}s_i^{m_i}\right]\]
   132	\[\alpha: \vZ_2^L \longrightarrow S\]
   133	\[(m_1,\dots,m_L)\mapsto \prod^L_{i=1}s_i^{m_i}.\]
   134	\end{prop}
   135	\begin{proof}
   136	The proof follows by observing that, since $\psi$ is $\sim$proper morphism, then also $\alpha_\sim$ is injective. Also $\alpha_\sim^\psi$ injective implies that $\alpha^\psi$ is injective. 
   137	Again, since $\psi$ is an injection, also $\alpha$ is injective.
   138	\end{proof}
   139	
   140	{As we have already pointed out}, this properties are necessary to keep the encryption meaningful. In the following we will see how it is possible to find non trivial examples of this construction. 
   141	
   142	
   143	Now, denote the image of  any map $f$ between sets by $\Im(f)$, and consider the following problems:
   144	\begin{problem}\label{problem1}
   145	Given $c\in \Im(\alpha^\psi_\sim)$ find $m$ such that $\alpha^\psi_\sim(m)=c$
   146	\end{problem}
   147	
   148	\begin{problem}\label{problem2}
   149	Given $c'\in \Im(\alpha_\sim)$ find $m$ such that $\alpha_\sim(m)=c'$.
   150	\end{problem}
   151	
   152	Let now $S$, be an $L$-cryptable monoid under a congruence $\sim$. Whenever a given triple $(S,\sim,\psi)$ is such that {Problem \ref{problem1}} is difficult, {Problem \ref{problem2}} is easy we define a cryptosystem as follows. Let
   153	 \[(S,\sim,L,\widetilde{\psi}([s_1]),\dots,\widetilde\psi([s_L]))\] 
   154	be the public key and
   155	 \[(\widetilde{\psi}^{-1}, s_1,\dots,s_L)\] 
   156	be the secret key, the main operations are given by
   157	\begin{itemize}
   158	\item{\emph{Encryption}: $E(m):=\alpha^\psi_\sim(m) = \prod^L_{i=1}\widetilde{\psi}([s_i])^{m_i}=: c$}
   159	\item{\emph{Decryption}: $D(c)$ is given by solving Problem \ref{problem2} for $c'=\widetilde{\psi}^{-1}(c)$.}
   160	\end{itemize}
   161	\begin{remark}
   162	The reader should observe that in the definition of the protocol we did not use the injectivity of $\psi$ nor the fact that $S/\sim$ is a quotient of a monoid $S$. This is nevertheless the case in all the examples of this protocol we could find, where Problem \ref{problem2} is easy since a \emph{suitable} lift to $S$ is given. Indeed, in practical situations the problem will be solved computing $(\alpha^{-1}\circ\Gamma)(c')$ where $\Gamma$ is a lift $S/\sim\longrightarrow S$ such that the following diagram 
   163	
   164	\begin{align}\label{liftdiagramma}
   165	\xymatrix{
   166	 \Z_2^L  \ar[r]^\alpha \ar[rd]_{\alpha_\sim}  & \Im(\alpha)  \\
   167	  & \Im (\alpha_\sim) \ar[u]_{\widehat{\Gamma}} }
   168	\end{align}
   169	commutes when $\widehat{\Gamma}:=\Gamma |_{\Im(\alpha_\sim)}$
   170	
   171	\end{remark}
   172	\begin{remark}\label{definforate}
   173	Notice that the information rate is given by $L/b$ where $b$ is the number of bits that are needed to represent an element of $S/\sim$
   174	\end{remark}
   175	
   176	In what follows we will show how the NSK protocol fits in this rather general framework, as well as brand new protocols involving polynomials over finite fields.
   177	
   178	
   179	\subsection{NSK as a particular instance}\label{particularcase}
   180	In this section we will show how the Naccache-Stern (NSK) protocol fits in our general framework, in the case $S=(\mathbb{Z},\cdot)$.
   181	
   182	Consider the prime ideal $P=\left<p\right>$ generated by a prime number $p\in\mathbb{Z}$. Let us denote by $\sim$ the congruence induced by the ideal $P$. Such a congruence is obviously of finite index. Let $v$ be a positive integer with $u=v^{-1}\mod{p-1}$, and let
   183	\[
   184	\begin{aligned} \psi\colon\mathbb{Z}&\longrightarrow \mathbb{Z}\\ a&\longmapsto a^v.\end{aligned}
   185	\]
   186	It can be easily checked that $\psi$ is a $\sim$proper morphism of $\vZ$.
   187	
   188	Now choose $L$ distinct prime numbers $p_i$ such that $\prod_{i=1}^L p_i<p$. 
   189	
   190	\begin{prop}The map
   191	\begin{equation}\begin{aligned}
   192	\alpha_\sim^\psi\colon \vZ_2^L&\longrightarrow\vZ/p\vZ\\
   193	(m_1,\dots,m_L)&\longmapsto\left[\prod\limits_{i=1}^Lp_i^{m_iv}\right]
   194	\end{aligned}\end{equation}
   195	is an injection and $(\vZ,\cdot)$ is therefore $L$-cryptable under the relation induced by the ideal generated by $p$.
   196	\end{prop} 
   197	\begin{proof}
   198	Assume that there exist two $L$-tuples $(m_1,\dots,m_L),(n_1,\dots,n_L)$ such that $\alpha_\sim^\psi(m_1,\dots,m_L)=\alpha_\sim^\psi(n_1,\dots,n_L)$, then:
   199	\[
   200	\left[\prod\limits_{i=1}^Lp_i^{m_iv}\right]=\left[\prod\limits_{i=1}^Lp_i^{n_iv}\right]\Rightarrow \left[\prod\limits_{i=1}^Lp_i^{m_iv}\right]^u=
   201	\left[\prod\limits_{i=1}^Lp_i^{n_iv}\right]^u\Leftrightarrow
   202	\left[\prod\limits_{i=1}^Lp_i^{m_i}\right]=\left[\prod\limits_{i=1}^Lp_i^{n_i}\right]
   203	\]
   204	in $\vZ/p\vZ$. Since $\prod_{i=1}^Lp_i^{m_i}$ and $\prod_{i=1}^Lp_i^{n_i}$ are smaller than $p$ we also have 
   205	\begin{equation}\label{canlift1}
   206	\prod\limits_{i=1}^Lp_i^{m_i}=\prod\limits_{i=1}^Lp_i^{n_i}
   207	\end{equation} 
   208	in the unique factorization domain $\vZ$, which implies $m_i=n_i\ \forall i$.
   209	\end{proof}
   210	
   211	\begin{remark}
   212	Notice that we are able to express equation \eqref{canlift1} because we can always consider the canonical representative $x\in\{0,\dots,p-1\}$ in the remainder class modulo $p$. This representative is also the only representative in $\Im(\alpha)$ by construction, and therefore we have a canonical lift satisfying \eqref{liftdiagramma}.
   213	\end{remark}
   214	
   215	
   216	
   217	\begin{remark}\label{NSKleak}
   218	The reader should observe that when $p=t+\prod_i p_i $ for $t$ small, than the information rate is maximal. Unfortunately in this case factoring $p-t$ is easy because $p-t$ is $p_L$-smooth and $p_L\ll p $, and this gives informations about the bare carriers $p_i$'s. Indeed in this case breaking the NSK protocol is not harder than solving the DLP for the $p_i$'s. Nevertheless the protocol remains interesting for additional features like \cite[Section 3]{NSK}.
   219	\end{remark}
   220	
   221	
   222	
