   224	\section{A polynomial version}\label{poly}
   225	In this section we  give a version of the protocol that works over $\vF_{q^d}$ instead of $\vZ/p\vZ$ in such a way that $q^d$ will be
   226	of the same order of magnitude than the size $p$ of the field $\vZ/p\vZ$ in the NSK but $q\ll p$.
   227	In this case the specific difficult problem we want to rely on is the following
   228	\begin{problem}
   229	Let $\vF$ be a finite field and $L\in\vN$.Given $y_1,\dots, y_L\in \vF$,
   230	\[\alpha: \vZ_2^L \longrightarrow \vF\]
   231	\[\alpha(m)=\prod_i y_i^{m_i}\]
   232	 and $c\in \Im(\alpha)$, find $m$ such that $\alpha(m)=c$.
   233	\end{problem}
   234	
   235	Let now $k=\vF_q$ and  $k[x]$ the polynomial ring in one variable over $k$.
   236	Let $h(x)$ be an irreducible element in $k[x]$ of degree $d$.
   237	Set $\sim$ to be the congruence associated to the ideal $H=\left<h(x)\right>$ generated by the irreducible polynomial $h(x)$. An efficient algorithm to find irreducible polynomials of fixed degree is given, for instance in \cite{POLLEG}.
   238	Set \[S=(k[x],\cdot)\] and \[S':=S/\sim\;=\left((k[x]/H)^*,\cdot\right)\]
   239	where $(k[x]/H)^*=(k[x]/H) \setminus \{0\}$.
   240	Fix $v,u\in \vN$ such that $\gcd{(v, |S'|) }=\gcd{(v, q^d-1) }=1$ and $uv\equiv 1 \mod |S'| $.
   241	Set 
   242	\[\begin{aligned}
   243	\widetilde{\psi}\colon S'&\longrightarrow S'\\
   244	[s]&\longmapsto [s^v].
   245	\end{aligned}\]
   246	
   247	
   248	\begin{remark}$\,$
   249	\begin{itemize}
   250	\item{$\widetilde{\psi}^{-1}\colon [z]\longmapsto [z]^u$}
   251	\item{$k[x]/H\cong \vF_{q^d}$ is again a finite field}
   252	\end{itemize}
   253	\end{remark}
   254	Let now $L\in \vN$ such that there exist $L$ distinct irreducible monic polynomials
   255	{$p_1,\dots, p_L\in\vF_q[x]$} with the property
   256	\begin{equation}\label{degreeproperty}
   257	\sum_{i=1}^L \deg{p_i}<d.
   258	\end{equation}
   259	Notice that in the present description of the protocol there are several different strategies to choose the polynomials; we will analyse the properties of some interesting choices in the following sections.
   260	
   261	Again, we have the encryption map:
   262	{\begin{prop}
   263	$(k[x],\cdot)$ is an $L$ cryptable monoid with the map
   264	\begin{equation}\begin{aligned}
   265	\alpha_\sim^\psi: \vZ_2^L &\longrightarrow S'\\
   266	m=(m_1,\dots,m_L)&\longmapsto \left[\prod^L_{i=1}p_i^{vm_i}\right].
   267	\end{aligned}\end{equation}
   268	\end{prop}
   269	\begin{proof} Definition \ref{defcryptable} requires that the map $\alpha_\sim^\psi$ be an injection. Assume
   270	\[\alpha_\sim^\psi(m_1,\dots,m_L)=\alpha_\sim^\psi(n_1,\dots,n_L)\]
   271	\[ \left[\prod^L_{i=1}p_i^{vm_i}\right]= \left[\prod^L_{i=1}p_i^{vn_i}\right].\]
   272	It follows
   273	\[ \left[\prod^L_{i=1}p_i^{vm_i}\right]^u= \left[\prod^L_{i=1}p_i^{vn_i}\right]^u\]
   274	\[ \left[\prod^L_{i=1}p_i^{m_i}\right]= \left[\prod^L_{i=1}p_i^{n_i}\right]\]
   275	where, in the last equation, we can assume no reduction has happened, since property
   276	(\ref{degreeproperty}) holds. Indeed
   277	\begin{equation}\label{canlift2} \prod^L_{i=1}p_i^{m_i}= \prod^L_{i=1}p_i^{n_i}.\end{equation}
   278	Recalling that $k[x]$ is a unique factorization domain we have $m_i=n_i\  \forall i$.
   279	\end{proof}
   280	}
   281	
   282	So our cyphered text is given by $c(x)=\alpha_\sim^\psi(m_1,\dots,m_L)$. The explicit decryption for this protocol is simply given by the polynomial division of the decyphered code $(c(x))^u$, that is to say 
   283	\begin{equation}\label{decryption}
   284	m_i=1 \Longleftrightarrow (c(x))^u=0 \mod p_i(x) .
   285	\end{equation}
   286	
   287	\begin{remark}
   288	We stress once again the fact that in obtaining equation \eqref{canlift2} we used the canonical lift 
   289	\begin{align*}
   290	\Gamma\colon &S/\sim\,\longrightarrow S \\
   291	& [f(x)]\longmapsto g(x)
   292	\end{align*}
   293	where, for any representative $l(x)\in[f(x)]$, $g(x)$ is the remainder of the division of $l(x)$ by $h(x)$ in $k[x]$, and it is obviously independent of the choice of $l(x)$.
   294	The decryption is effectively performed in $\Im(\alpha)$ and the solution to Problem \ref{problem2} is then given by $(\alpha^{-1}\circ \Gamma)(c(x)^u)$.
   295	\end{remark}
   296	
   297	The information rate $\mathcal{I}=L/\deg(h)\log_2(q)$ depends on the choice of the carrier polynomials. We will explain later how to maximise this value.
   298	
   299	\begin{remark}
   300	Once the $p_i$'s are fixed the top information rate for this protocol is obtained when we choose $h(x)$ such that
   301	\begin{equation}\label{topinfocondition}
   302	\sum\limits_{i=1}^L\deg{p_i}=\deg{h}-1.
   303	\end{equation}
   304	Indeed the information rate can always be maximised since it is always possible to choose $h(x)$ in $k[x]$ such that \eqref{topinfocondition} is satisfied (cf. Remark \ref{NSKleak}) without allowing for a straightforward reduction to a DLP. This case will be analysed in detail in \ref{efficiency}.
   305	\end{remark}
