   615	
   616	\subsubsection{Some precautions to avoid subgroup-like attacks}\label{security}
   617	The security of this protocol is strictly related to the size of the degree of $h$ and, as a consequence, to  the range of degrees that the carriers can have. Indeed, when the carriers are chosen within a large set, the attacker will not have chances (in terms of a brute force attack) to find the $p_i$'s to set up a discrete logarithm problem for the pair $(p_i,p_i^s)$ for  any $i$.
   618	
   619	As a matter of fact, the knowledge of $h$ will only lead to the following information on the degrees:
   620	\[\deg(h)=\sum_i{\deg(p_i)}+1.\] 
   621	This is not the case when working with integers and primes in $\vZ/p\vZ$, where we can always assume that the prime factors are known
   622	when $p\simeq \prod_i p_i$.
   623	
   624	We first sketch a subgroup like attack in the most \emph{unsafe} case.
   625	Let $G$ be an abelian group and $p^v_1,\dots,p^v_L$ be carriers, as in Section \ref{poly}.
   626	Let the order of $p^v_i$ in $G$ be $n_i$ and suppose $\gcd{(n_i,n_j)}=1$ for $i\neq j$.
   627	Let now
   628	\[M_j=n_1\cdots n_{j-1}\cdot n_{j+1}\cdots n_L.\]
   629	It is easy to observe that, for a generic cyphertext $c$, $m_j=1$ if and only if $c^{M_j}\neq 1$. As it is elementary to observe, this leads to decryption in $L$ steps. Moreover, it can also be adapted to work when the condition $\gcd{(n_i,n_j)}=1$ is just partially fulfilled. {In this case, indeed, only partial information on the text can be extracted.}
   630	
   631	
   632	
   633	Consider now the decomposition in cyclic subgroups of the multiplicative group of the finite field $(\vF_{q^d})^*$. In order to avoid subgroup-like attacks on the cyphertext we will require all the $p_i$'s to be generators of the same subgroup of large order. This will lead to certain requirements on $q^d-1$.
   634	
   635	The most natural choice to solve this problem is asking that the degree $d$ of the reducing polynomial $h(x)$ be constrained by the following:
   636	\begin{equation}\label{chone}
   637	r:=\frac{q^d-1}{q-1}\ {\mathit{is\ prime}}.
   638	\end{equation}
   639	
   640	Now we have to choose the $p_i$'s such that
   641	\begin{equation}\label{chtwo}
   642	p_i(x)^r\neq 1 \mod{h(x)}\quad \forall i\in\{1,\dots,L\}.
   643	\end{equation}
   644	When these conditions are satisfied, all the $p_i$'s are generators for $(\vF_{q^d})^*$.
   645	
   646	
   647	\subsection{``Chinese remainder'' version}\label{CRT}
   648	In what follows we will present another example of a protocol that fits the general picture, which stems on the well known chinese remainder theorem. To do this, let us introduce a large prime power $q$ and a natural number $L\in \vN$. Consider now the monoid $S=(\vF_q^{L+1})^*$, with the multiplication defined componentwise, and the set $R=\{r_1,\dots,r_{L+1}\}\subseteq\vF_q$.
   649	
   650	Let $\alpha_i\in \vF_q\backslash R\quad\forall i\in\{1,\dots,L\}$ and choose two large integers $u,v$ such that $uv=1\mod{(q-1)}$. Compute the following list of vectors $p_i\in(\vF_q^{L+1})^*$ as
   651	\[
   652	(g_i)_j:=(r_j-\alpha_i)
   653	\]
   654	\[
   655	(p_i)_j:=(g_i)^v.
   656	\]
   657	Let 
   658	\[((\vF_q^{L+1})^*, \{p_1,\dots,p_L\})\]
   659	be the public key and
   660	\[(\{g_1,\dots, g_L\}, \{r_1\dots r_L\})\]
   661	be the secret key.
   662	Let
   663	\[F:\vZ_2^L \longrightarrow S\]
   664	\[(m_1,\dots,m_L)\mapsto  \prod_{i=1}^L p_i^{m_i}\]
   665	be the encryption map.
   666	\begin{remark}
   667	Observe that the information rate is
   668	\[\frac{L}{(L+1)\log_2(q)}.\]
   669	\end{remark}
   670	\begin{prop}
   671	$F$ is an injection.
   672	\end{prop}
   673	\begin{proof}
   674	We define a polynomial on $\vF_q[x]$ by
   675	\[
   676	h_R(x):=\prod_{i=1}^{L+1}(x-r_i)
   677	\]
   678	whose set of zeros coincide with $R$.
   679	We will prove the proposition by showing how to compute the inverse over the image of $F$ using $h(x)$, i.e. we will show how to uniquely decrypt any cyphertext $c\in \Im(F)$ using the secret key.
   680	Let 
   681	\[\psi: S \longrightarrow S\]
   682	\[x\mapsto x^v\]
   683	\[G: \vF_q[x]/h_R(x) \stackrel{\mathrm{CRT}}{\longrightarrow} \vF_q^L\]
   684	\[k(x)\mapsto (k(r_1),\dots,k(r_L))\]
   685	and 
   686	\[\Gamma: \vF_q[x]/h_R(x) \longrightarrow \vF_q[x]\]
   687	be the canonical lift.
   688	The decryption map $D$ is given by checking $\Gamma( G^{-1}(\psi^{-1}(x)))$ modulo $g_i(x)=(x-\alpha_i)$: whenever it is zero it means $m_i=1$, where $ \psi^{-1}(x)= x^u$. Observe that the decryption is well defined: the map
   689	\[\alpha_\sim^\psi: \vZ^L_2\longrightarrow \vF_q[x]/(h_R(x))\]
   690	is clearly injective (and then $\alpha_\sim$ is, by Proposition \ref{injalpha}) since the product of all the $g_i(x)$ has degree $L<L+1$.
   691	Observe that $\sim$ is as usual the relation induced by the ideal of $h_R(x)$.
   692	\end{proof}
   693	
